<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Užice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Titovo Užice</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Užice is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on the Đetinja river. In the 14th century it was the seat of the regional lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-altomanovic">Nikola Altomanović</a>, who was captured there by Prince Lazar and Tvrtko of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1373. Later held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, it gave its name in 1941 to the short-lived Partisan Republic of Užice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzice.jpg" alt="Užice" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uzice_pan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
